  1→# Joy of VEX: Ramps & Parameters
  2→
  3→> Tutorial examples from The Joy of VEX video series by Matt Estela.
  4→> Source: https://www.youtube.com/@MattEstela
  5→
  6→## Quick Reference
  7→```vex
  8→chv('scalevec');  // Channel Reference Scale Vector
  9→@N = chf('scale');  // Setting Normals with Channel Reference
 10→vector uv = chv("uv");  // Declaring UV vector parameter
 11→```
 12→
 13→## Parameter Controls
 14→
 15→### Channel references in expressions [[Ep1, 40:20](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=2420s)]
 16→```vex
 17→@Cd = float(@ptnum)/100;
 18→
 19→@Cd = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 20→
 21→@Cd = float(@ptnum)/ch();
 22→```
 23→Demonstrates replacing hardcoded numeric values with channel references using the ch() function to create UI parameters. This allows interactive control of values without editing code, by referencing parameter names like 'scale' or auto-creating parameters when the function is left incomplete.
 24→
 25→### Channel References in VEX [[Ep1, 41:46](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=2506s)]
 26→```vex
 27→@Cd = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 28→```
 29→Demonstrates using the ch() function to reference a parameter named 'scale' from the VEX wrangle node interface, allowing dynamic control over color values. The ch() function takes a string parameter name and returns its value, which can then be used in expressions. Quotes denote strings in VEX, and the parameter can be auto-created using the wrangle's UI button if it doesn't exist.
 30→
 31→### Channel references for parameter control [[Ep1, 43:18](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=2598s)]
 32→```vex
 33→@Cd = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 34→
 35→@Cd = sin(@ptnum);
 36→```
 37→Demonstrates using the ch() function to reference a user interface parameter ('scale') that controls color output, allowing for interactive art direction without rewriting code. Shows progression from dividing point numbers by a slider value to using sine function for different color patterns.
 38→
 39→### Variables and Channel References [[Ep1, 50:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=3002s)]
 40→```vex
 41→@Cd = sin(@ptnum);
 42→
 43→@Cd = sin(@ptnum/100);
 44→
 45→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/100);
 46→
 47→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)*ch('scale'));
 48→
 49→float foo = float(@ptnum)*ch('scale');
 50→@Cd = sin(foo);
 51→
 52→float foo = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 53→@Cd = sin(foo);
 54→```
 55→Demonstrates storing intermediate calculations in variables rather than inline expressions. The example shows extracting the channel reference calculation into a variable named 'foo', which makes the code more readable and easier to debug. This progression also illustrates common debugging practices like fixing parenthesis errors.
 56→
 57→### Variables and Channel References [[Ep1, 50:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=3012s)]
 58→```vex
 59→@Cd = sin(@ptnum);
 60→
 61→@Cd = sin(@ptnum/100);
 62→
 63→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/100);
 64→
 65→@Cd = sin(float(@ptnum)/ch('scale'));
 66→
 67→float foo = float(@ptnum)/ch('scale');
 68→@Cd = sin(foo);
 69→```
 70→This demonstrates the progression from hardcoded values to parameterized expressions by introducing variables and channel references. The point number is cast to float, divided by a channel parameter 'scale', stored in a variable 'foo', then passed to sin() to set color. This shows how to create reusable, adjustable code by extracting complex expressions into named variables and connecting them to UI parameters.
 71→
 72→### Scaling distance with channel reference [[Ep1, 72:54](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4374s)]
 73→```vex
 74→float d = length(@P);
 75→d *= ch('scale');
 76→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 77→```
 78→Calculates distance from origin and scales it using a channel parameter for control. The scaled distance drives a color value through a sine function normalized to 0-1 range. This demonstrates why multiplying by parameters is preferred over division to avoid potential divide-by-zero errors.
 79→
 80→### Distance with Channel-Referenced Center [[Ep1, 80:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4802s)]
 81→```vex
 82→vector center = chv('center');
 83→float d = distance(@P, center);
 84→d *= ch('scale');
 85→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 86→```
 87→This code refactors the distance-based color pattern to use a vector parameter for the center point, allowing dynamic control via a channel reference. The distance calculation and sine-based color mapping remain the same, but now the center point can be adjusted interactively through the parameter interface rather than being hardcoded in the VEX code.
 88→
 89→### Vector Channel Parameter for Center [[Ep1, 80:16](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=4816s)]
 90→```vex
 91→float d = distance(@P, {3,0,3});
 92→d *= ch('scale');
 93→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 94→
 95→float d = distance(@P, {1,0,1});
 96→d += ch('scale');
 97→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
 98→
 99→vector center = chv('center');
100→float d = distance(@P, center);
101→d += ch('scale');
102→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
103→```
104→Demonstrates the progression from hardcoded center points to a parameterized vector channel. The final version uses chv('center') to create a vector parameter that allows interactive control of the concentric ring pattern's origin point, replacing the hardcoded {3,0,3} and {1,0,1} positions.
105→
106→### Parameterized Position and Color [[Ep1, 84:18](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5058s)]
107→```vex
108→vector pos = @P * chv('fancy_scale');
109→vector center = chv('center');
110→vector d = normalize(pos - center);
111→d *= ch('scale');
112→@Cd = (sin(d) + 1) * 0.5;
113→```
114→Creates a parameterized color effect by scaling position with a vector channel reference, computing the normalized direction from a center point, and converting a sine wave distance calculation to color values. The code demonstrates replacing hard-coded values with channel references (chv) to make the effect adjustable via UI parameters.
115→
116→### Channel References for Interactive Parameters [[Ep1, 84:50](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5090s)]
117→```vex
118→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
119→vector center = chv('center');
120→float d = distance(pos, center);
121→d *= ch('scale');
122→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
123→```
124→Demonstrates converting hardcoded values to channel references using ch() and chv() functions to create interactive UI parameters. The 'fancyscale' vector parameter allows scaling position coordinates independently in X, Y, Z before distance calculation, enabling real-time manipulation of concentric color rings. Setting default values to (1,1,1) maintains the original appearance while providing artistic control.
125→
126→### Channel Parameters for Interactive Pattern Control [[Ep1, 85:14](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5114s)]
127→```vex
128→vector pos = @P * chv('anyscale');
129→vector center = chv('center');
130→float d = distance(pos, center);
131→d *= ch('scale');
132→@Cd = (sin(d) + 1) * 0.5;
133→```
134→Demonstrates adding channel parameter references to VEX code for interactive control of a radial ring pattern generator. By using chv() for vector parameters and ch() for float parameters, the pattern's scale, center position, and ring spacing can be adjusted in real-time through the UI without modifying code.
135→
136→### Channel References for Radial Patterns [[Ep1, 85:50](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5150s)]
137→```vex
138→vector pos = @P + chv('fancyscale');
139→vector center = chv('center');
140→float d = distance(pos, center);
141→d *= ch('scale');
142→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
143→```
144→Demonstrates creating a controllable radial ring pattern by exposing multiple parameters through channel references. Uses distance from a movable center point, scales it, and applies sine function to generate color values, showing how strategic parameter placement enables quick iteration on procedural patterns.
145→
146→### Radial Pattern with Channel References [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 85:54](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5154s)]
147→```vex
148→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
149→vector center = chv('center');
150→f@distance = distance(pos, center);
151→@distance *= ch('scaling');
152→@Cd = (sin(@distance) + 1) * 0.5;
153→```
154→Demonstrates creating a controllable radial ring pattern by exposing channel parameters through chv() and ch() for scaling and center position. The pattern uses distance from a center point combined with sine wave calculations to drive color values, creating movable and squishable rings.
155→
156→### Vector vs Float Channel Parameters [[Ep1, 86:34](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5194s)]
157→```vex
158→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
159→vector center = chv('center');
160→float d = distance(pos, center);
161→d *= ch('_scale');
162→@Cd = sin(d) * 1 * 0.5;
163→```
164→Demonstrates the difference between using ch() for float parameters and chv() for vector parameters when creating UI controls. The code calculates color based on distance from a center point, scaling position and distance values using channel references. Shows how changing a parameter type from float to vector (ch to chv) requires recreating the parameter if it already exists.
165→
166→### Changing Parameter Types and Spare Parameters [[Ep1, 86:56](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5216s)]
167→```vex
168→vector pos = @P + chv('fancyscale');
169→vector center = chv('center');
170→@Cd = length(pos, center);
171→@P *= chv('scale');
172→@Cd = (sin(@P))*0.5;
173→```
174→Demonstrates the use of channel reference functions (ch vs chv) for accessing parameters, and addresses the workflow for changing parameter types after they've been created. When you need to change a parameter type (e.g., from float to vector), you can delete all spare parameters and remake them, or manually delete individual spare parameters.
175→
176→### Channel Parameters and Type Conversion [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 87:00](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5220s)]
177→```vex
178→vector pos = @P + chv('fancyscale');
179→vector center = chv('center');
180→float d = distance(pos, center);
181→d *= chf('scale');
182→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
183→```
184→Demonstrates how to use channel reference functions to expose parameters in the UI, including the importance of using the correct type (chv for vectors, chf for floats). The code calculates distance from a center point and uses it to create a sinusoidal color pattern. Also shows that changing parameter types requires deleting and recreating spare parameters.
185→
186→### Converting Parameters to Vector Type [[Ep1, 87:14](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5234s)]
187→```vex
188→vector pos = @P * chv('scale');
189→vector center = chv('center');
190→float dist = distance(pos, center);
191→dist *= chv('scale');
192→@Cd = (sin(dist) * 1) * 0.5;
193→```
194→Demonstrates how to manage spare parameters when converting between types (float to vector). When a channel reference like chv() needs to change type, you can delete all spare parameters and let them regenerate, or selectively delete and recreate individual parameters to match the new type.
195→
196→### Managing Spare Parameters [[Ep1, 87:24](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5244s)]
197→```vex
198→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
199→vector center = chv('center');
200→f@dist = distance(pos, center);
201→@dist *= chv('scale');
202→@Cd = (sin(@dist)+1)*0.5;
203→
204→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
205→vector center = chv('center');
206→f@d = distance(pos, center);
207→@d *= chv('scale');
208→@Cd = (sin(@d)+1)*0.5;
209→```
210→Two variants of the same distance-based color calculation demonstrating spare parameter workflow. When changing parameter types (like converting 'scale' from float to vector), you can either delete all spare parameters and recreate them, or delete individual parameters and re-add them to update their type definitions.
211→
212→### Spare Parameters and Type Casting [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 87:26](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5246s)]
213→```vex
214→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
215→vector center = chv('center');
216→vector d = distance(pos, center);
217→d *= chv('scale');
218→@Cd = (sin(d)+1)*0.5;
219→```
220→Demonstrates creating channel reference spare parameters and handling implicit type casting issues. The code calculates distance from a center point and uses it to create a color pattern, with discussion of properly casting between vector and float types when using distance() and managing spare parameters through deletion and recreation.
221→
222→### Parameter Type Casting and Distance [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 88:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5292s)]
223→```vex
224→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
225→vector center = chv('center');
226→vector delta = pos - center;
227→float d = length(delta);
228→d *= ch('scalePos');
229→@Cd = sin(d * 3) * 0.5;
230→```
231→Demonstrates computing distance from a point to a center using vector subtraction and length(), then using that distance to drive color with a sine wave. Also highlights the importance of explicit type casting when creating parameters - if you need to change a parameter's type (e.g., from vector to float), you must delete and recreate it rather than just clicking the create parameter button again.
232→
233→### Parameter Type Management [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 88:22](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5302s)]
234→```vex
235→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
236→vector center = chv('center');
237→vector delta = pos - center;
238→float dist = length(delta);
239→@Cd = sin(dist) + 0.5;
240→```
241→Demonstrates the importance of properly managing spare parameter types in VEX. Once a parameter is created with a specific type (float vs vector), it must be deleted and recreated to change its type, as simply modifying the channel reference won't automatically update the parameter interface.
242→
243→### Channel References and Art Directability [Needs Review] [[Ep1, 99:38](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9gB1zBa9Lg4&t=5978s)]
244→```vex
245→vector pos = @P * chv('fancyscale');
246→vector center = chv('center');
247→float d = distance(pos, center);
248→@Cd = ch('scale');
249→@Cd = fit(sin(d*@Frame), -1, 1, 0, 1);
250→
251→foo *= 5;
252→
253→foo = foo * 1 + 1 / @Cd.x * @N.y;
254→
255→foo *= 1; // set range
256→foo += 1; // make sure values never get below 0
257→foo /= @Cd.x; // reduce range to within red value
258→foo *= @N.y; // sanitize normal on y
259→
260→foo *= 1; // set range
261→foo += 1; // make sure values never get below 0
262→foo /= @Cd.x; // reduce range to within red value
263→foo *= @N.y; // sanitize normal on y
264→```
265→Demonstrates using channel references (ch() and chv()) to create art-directable VEX effects by exposing parameters that can be animated or adjusted interactively. The code shows distance-based color manipulation using fit() and sin() functions combined with @Frame for animation, along with various normalization techniques using color and normal attributes.
266→
267→### Channel Parameters and Clamping [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 100:12](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=6012s)]
268→```vex
269→float d = length(@P);
270→d = ch('pre_scale');
271→d = clamp(d, ch('post_scale'), d);
272→d += ch('post_scale');
273→@Cd = d;
274→```
275→This code demonstrates controlling values using channel parameters with ch() function and the clamp() function to constrain values within a range. The distance value is replaced by pre_scale parameter, clamped using post_scale as minimum, then offset by post_scale before assigning to color, creating a controlled stepped or striated effect.
276→
277→## chramp Usage
278→
279→### Remapping Values with Ramps [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 101:34](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=6094s)]
280→```vex
281→float d = length(@P);
282→d = ch('pre_scale');
283→d -= chramp('my_stepped_ramp', d);
284→d *= ch('post_scale');
285→@P.y = d;
286→```
287→This code demonstrates a common VEX workflow of taking values through different ranges and transformations. It calculates distance, applies pre-scaling via a channel parameter, subtracts a ramped value using chramp(), applies post-scaling, and assigns the result to the Y position, illustrating how to fit and manipulate values for desired effects.
288→
289→## Parameter Controls
290→
291→### Fitting and Clamping with Channels [[Ep2, 29:02](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=1742s)]
292→```vex
293→float d = length(@P);
294→float imin = ch('fit_in_min');
295→float imax = ch('fit_in_max');
296→d = fit(d, imin, imax, 1, 0);
297→d = clamp(d, 0.5, 1);
298→@P.y = d;
299→```
300→Demonstrates using fit() to remap distance values from a custom input range (controlled by channel parameters) to an output range of 1 to 0, then applying an additional clamp() to restrict the result between 0.5 and 1 before assigning to the Y position. This creates a controlled vertical displacement based on distance from the origin with parameter-driven input bounds.
301→
302→### Channel-Driven Fit Range [[Ep2, 33:32](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2012s)]
303→```vex
304→float d = length(@P);
305→float inmin = ch("fit_in_min");
306→float inmax = ch("fit_in_max");
307→float outmin = ch("fit_out_min");
308→float outmax = ch("fit_out_max");
309→d = fit(d, inmin, inmax, outmin, outmax);
310→@P.y = d;
311→```
312→Creates channel references for all fit() function parameters, allowing interactive control of input and output ranges from the parameter interface. This demonstrates how to expose VEX calculations to the UI by using ch() to read parameter values and apply them to geometry transformations based on distance from origin.
313→
314→### Channel-driven fit with UI parameters [[Ep2, 34:58](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2098s)]
315→```vex
316→float d = length(@P);
317→float imin = ch("fit_in_min");
318→float imax = ch("fit_in_max");
319→float outmin = ch("fit_out_min");
320→float outmax = ch("fit_out_max");
321→d = fit(d, imin, imax, outmin, outmax);
322→@Cd.y = d;
323→```
324→Demonstrates creating a user interface for the fit function by exposing all input and output range parameters as channels. Uses ch() to read parameter values and allows interactive control of the remapping ranges, with spare parameters automatically created from the channel references.
325→
326→### Fit Function with Channel Parameters [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 40:20](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2420s)]
327→```vex
328→float angle = ch('fit_in_min');
329→float imagex = ch('fit_in_max');
330→float imagey = ch('fit_out_min');
331→float outmin = ch('fit_out_max');
332→float outmax = outmin;
333→@P.x = fit(angle, imagex, imagey, outmin, outmax);
334→@P.y = angle;
335→```
336→This code demonstrates using the fit() function with channel references to remap values from one range to another, setting the X position based on the remapped value and Y position to the original input angle. The channel parameters provide UI controls for the input and output ranges of the fit operation.
337→
338→## chramp Usage
339→
340→### Ramp-driven Y displacement [[Ep2, 43:46](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2626s)]
341→```vex
342→float d = length(@P);
343→d *= fit(scale);
344→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
345→```
346→This code calculates the distance from the origin for each point, scales it using a fit operation, then uses that scaled distance to sample a custom ramp parameter and assign the result to the Y position. The technique allows for ramp-controlled radial displacement patterns that can be adjusted interactively.
347→
348→### Distance to Ramp Mapping [[Ep2, 44:10](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2650s)]
349→```vex
350→float d = length(@P);
351→d = d * (v@scale);
352→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
353→```
354→Uses the length() function to calculate distance from origin, scales it by a vector parameter, then uses chramp() to map the scaled distance value through a ramp parameter to control the Y position of points. This demonstrates how to normalize distance values to a 0-1 range for ramp lookups to create interesting height variations.
355→
356→### Ramp Parameter with Distance [[Ep2, 45:22](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2722s)]
357→```vex
358→float d = length(v@P);
359→d *= ch('scale');
360→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
361→```
362→This code calculates the distance from the origin using length(), scales it with a channel parameter, and then uses that scaled distance to sample a ramp parameter called 'myramp' which drives the Y position of each point. The chramp() function evaluates the ramp parameter at the given distance value, allowing for artistic control over point displacement based on distance.
363→
364→### Using chramp with distance [[Ep2, 45:26](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2726s)]
365→```vex
366→float d = length(@P);
367→d *= ch('scale');
368→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
369→```
370→Creates a ramp-based deformation by calculating the distance from the origin using length(@P), scaling it with a channel parameter, and then using that distance value to evaluate a ramp parameter that drives the Y position of points. The chramp function takes a ramp parameter name ('myramp') and evaluates it at the scaled distance value to create smooth vertical displacement.
371→
372→### Ramp Lookup with Distance Scaling [[Ep2, 46:20](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=2780s)]
373→```vex
374→float d = length(@P);
375→d *= ch("scale");
376→@P.y = chramp("myRamp", d);
377→@P.y *= ch("height");
378→```
379→Calculates point distance from origin and uses it as a lookup value into a ramp parameter, scaled by a channel slider. The ramp output is applied to the Y position and multiplied by a height control, allowing interactive adjustment of both the distance scale and the final displacement magnitude.
380→
381→### Chramp Modulo Behavior Change [[Ep2, 50:20](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3020s)]
382→```vex
383→float d = length(@P);
384→d *= ch('scale');
385→d += @Time;
386→@P.y = chramp('my-ramp', d);
387→@P.y *= ch('height');
388→```
389→Uses chramp() to sample a ramp parameter based on distance from origin plus time, then applies that value to the Y position with a height multiplier. The discussion focuses on a feature change where chramp() no longer automatically modulos values, meaning values outside 0-1 range won't repeat the ramp pattern.
390→
391→### Ramp-based height displacement [[Ep2, 50:26](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3026s)]
392→```vex
393→float d = length(@P);
394→d *= ch("scale");
395→d = abs(d);
396→d = sin(d);
397→d = fit(d, -1, 1, 0, 1);
398→@P.y = chramp("my-ramp", d);
399→@P.y *= ch("height");
400→```
401→This code creates a radial displacement pattern by calculating distance from origin, applying trigonometric transformation, and using a ramp parameter to control the vertical displacement of points. The chramp() function samples a color ramp UI parameter which no longer automatically wraps/modulos values as it did in earlier Houdini versions.
402→
403→### Ramp-driven height with distance fitting [Needs Review] [[Ep2, 51:04](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3064s)]
404→```vex
405→float d = length(@P);
406→d *= ch('scale');
407→d = fit(d, 1, 1, 0, 1);
408→@P.y = chramp('myRamp', d);
409→@P.y *= ch('height');
410→```
411→Calculates distance from origin, scales and remaps it using fit(), then uses the remapped value to sample a ramp parameter that drives vertical displacement. The fit() function normalizes the distance range before sampling the ramp, allowing for controlled height distribution based on radial distance.
412→
413→### Ramp-driven height with channel offset [[Ep2, 53:16](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3196s)]
414→```vex
415→float d = length(@P);
416→d *= ch('scale');
417→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
418→@P.y += ch('height');
419→```
420→This code calculates the distance from the origin, scales it with a channel parameter, and uses that scaled distance to sample a ramp parameter that drives the Y position. An additional height channel parameter is added as an offset to vertically shift the entire ramp-driven surface.
421→
422→### Ramp-Based Height Displacement [[Ep2, 53:18](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3198s)]
423→```vex
424→float d = length(@P);
425→d *= ch('scale');
426→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
427→@P.y *= ch('height');
428→```
429→This code displaces point height based on distance from origin using a ramp for shape control. The distance is calculated, scaled by a channel parameter, then used to sample a color ramp which determines the Y displacement, finally multiplied by a height multiplier for amplitude control.
430→
431→### Ramp Parameter Modulation [[Ep2, 53:22](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OyjB5ZifIuU&t=3202s)]
432→```vex
433→float d = length(@P);
434→d *= ch('scale');
435→@Cd = chramp('myramp', d);
436→@P.y = chramp('myramp', d);
437→```
